  "summary": "DOAC dose review, week of 10/19/2026 (RBC-PHARM-07). I reviewed all 7 patients; each one has a note citing the records used.\n\n- pt-0061 Irene Castellano: apixaban 2.5 → 5 mg BID (mr-t0001). Age 74, 78 kg, SCr 1.02, so no dose criteria met. Itraconazole course ended 08/14 per the ID letter.\n- pt-0003 Frank Delgado: rivaroxaban stays at 20 mg daily (mr-t0009). I first cut it to 15 mg based on the St. Brigid SCr of 160 µmol/L (CrCl 44). The lab then corrected that result to 118 µmol/L (calibration fault), giving CrCl 59.9, so I reversed the cut. Correction note doc-t0010.\n- pt-0041 Raymond Duquette: apixaban 5 → 2.5 mg BID (mr-t0005). No dose criteria met, but he has an active itraconazole order. Itraconazole not changed.\n- pt-0062 Norman Fairweather: apixaban 5 mg BID correct, no change. Only age 82 counts. ED creatinine arrived during the review: 150 µmol/L on 10/21, then 100 µmol/L (1.13 mg/dL) on 10/22. I used the later value.\n- pt-0038 Eugene Moravec: rivaroxaban 15 → 20 mg daily (mr-t0007). On actual weight (96 kg) with SCr 1.45, age 79, CrCl is 56.1. The May reduction had used ideal body weight, which the policy doesn't allow. He sent a home-scale weight of 185 lb; per policy a self-reported weight doesn't count, so no change. I suggested a measured weight at his next visit (addendum doc-t0021).\n- pt-0043 Marjorie Pellegrino: rivaroxaban 20 → 15 mg daily (mr-t0011). New SCr 1.25 on 10/22 (resulted this morning), female, 71 kg, age 75: CrCl 43.6.\n- pt-0002 Harold Vance: net no change, apixaban 2.5 mg BID (mr-t0015). Only age 82 counts (64.9 kg, SCr 1.21), so I first raised him to 5 mg BID. A St. Brigid Dermatology letter dated 10/22 then arrived saying they started itraconazole that day for 12 weeks. That requires 2.5 mg BID, so I set it back. Correction note doc-t0016.\n\nI didn't change any other medications. I closed the 5 inbox items that arrived during the review, each with a comment. Older inbox items for unrelated patients were left alone. No one had both qualifying criteria and an interacting drug, so no prescriber warning was needed.\n\nFollow-up: re-review the apixaban dose for pt-0041 and pt-0002 when their itraconazole courses end. I didn't create reminder tasks for this; for pt-0002 that's around mid-January 2027.",
